  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2 over x, z from -2 to 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. It is steeper in x, so steps overshoot there first. Without noise a step overshoots when the learning rate is above 0.4 and swings grow when it is above 0.8.",
    "Each step adds Gaussian noise to the true slope. Its spread is 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is largest at 1 and exactly 0 at 64. Each step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows use one scale: 2 × the true step length. The orange arrow is the next step (−rate × noisy slope), the way the ball moves. The grey arrow is −rate × true slope.",
    "A run is finished after 40 steps. It is diverged once the loss goes above 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The trail is clamped to the bowl's edge, but the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The noise comes from a fixed seed. Each new run takes the next noise values, and Reset replays the sequence from the start. The trail holds the starting point plus one point per step."
  ]
}